WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.493 --> 00:00:05.986
(applause)

00:00:07.562 --> 00:00:08.684
- Thank you.

00:00:09.513 --> 00:00:10.892
Welcome.

00:00:11.522 --> 00:00:13.489
I scoured this place last week

00:00:13.489 --> 00:00:15.205
to see what's going on here.

00:00:16.329 --> 00:00:17.991
I still have an audience, that's good.

00:00:17.991 --> 00:00:21.221
So this is not about football, I'm sorry to tell you.

00:00:22.831 --> 00:00:27.438
It's about ideas, and it's about my life.

00:00:27.438 --> 00:00:29.230
And it's about your life.

00:00:29.241 --> 00:00:31.215
So, as Tina says, I actually started

00:00:31.215 --> 00:00:33.508
as a pretty straight mechanical engineer

00:00:33.508 --> 00:00:35.547
out of New York City.

00:00:35.832 --> 00:00:37.118
I still have the accent.

00:00:37.118 --> 00:00:39.665
I've only been off the boat 50-some odd years,

00:00:39.665 --> 00:00:41.323
so it takes a while.

00:00:41.807 --> 00:00:45.924
And I have three degrees in mechanical engineering

00:00:45.924 --> 00:00:47.574
that I actually earned.

00:00:47.758 --> 00:00:49.768
And, after my PhD,

00:00:49.768 --> 00:00:52.806
I actually was supposed to stay at Columbia,

00:00:52.806 --> 00:00:55.248
but I got a free vacation to California.

00:00:55.248 --> 00:00:57.361
And, since my wife hated New York,

00:00:57.361 --> 00:00:59.581
we ended up staying at Stanford.

00:00:59.582 --> 00:01:02.047
And I've been here since 1962,

00:01:02.047 --> 00:01:04.770
which is before a lot of you had been born.

00:01:05.082 --> 00:01:06.601
So, I've been here a long time.

00:01:06.601 --> 00:01:08.472
And the thing to understand is,

00:01:08.472 --> 00:01:12.588
the Stanford and the Silicon Valley I came to is very,

00:01:12.588 --> 00:01:16.454
very different, or was very different than it is today.

00:01:16.506 --> 00:01:20.904
And one of the things that struck me when I was here is

00:01:20.904 --> 00:01:23.668
A, there was there was the Human Potential Movement

00:01:23.668 --> 00:01:25.367
and all that, so I learned there were other things

00:01:25.367 --> 00:01:26.835
than machines and robots.

00:01:26.835 --> 00:01:27.995
There were people.

00:01:27.995 --> 00:01:30.932
And I got interested in that, in creativity.

00:01:31.382 --> 00:01:34.217
And I started to think about teaching,

00:01:34.217 --> 00:01:36.577
in addition to the things I knew something about,

00:01:36.577 --> 00:01:39.677
the things I wish I had learned in school myself.

00:01:39.940 --> 00:01:42.537
And one of the things I noticed was

00:01:42.537 --> 00:01:46.155
that people talked a lot about starting their own companies.

00:01:46.468 --> 00:01:48.285
And most of them didn't.

00:01:48.285 --> 00:01:51.165
In the Design Program where I was an ME,

00:01:51.273 --> 00:01:54.384
most of the students ended up working for Hewlett-Packard

00:01:54.384 --> 00:01:56.843
or maybe Ampex, which doesn't exist anymore,

00:01:56.843 --> 00:01:58.822
or Raychem, which doesn't exist.

00:01:58.822 --> 00:02:00.900
But they went to these large companies.

00:02:00.900 --> 00:02:02.645
And they all had this kind of pipe dream,

00:02:02.645 --> 00:02:05.277
this fantasy of starting their own company.

00:02:05.277 --> 00:02:07.560
And that kind of, I didn't really care,

00:02:07.560 --> 00:02:08.980
but it actually reminded me

00:02:08.980 --> 00:02:13.965
of a play called The Iceman Cometh, an O'Neill play.

00:02:14.303 --> 00:02:16.766
And in that play these people are in the bar,

00:02:16.766 --> 00:02:19.168
and the whole play takes place in the bar.

00:02:19.512 --> 00:02:22.116
And they talk about leaving the bar,

00:02:22.159 --> 00:02:24.949
and the whole play, nobody leaves the bar.

00:02:24.949 --> 00:02:27.191
And somehow this whole idea of starting a company

00:02:27.191 --> 00:02:29.925
reminded me of that, and it bothered me a lot.

00:02:29.925 --> 00:02:33.957
So I decided to start a course, which had in it two,

00:02:33.957 --> 00:02:36.887
you could do a project, and the project involved

00:02:36.887 --> 00:02:38.669
one of two topics.

00:02:38.669 --> 00:02:42.600
One was, do something you've never done before in your life.

00:02:42.946 --> 00:02:46.202
And the other was, get rid of a problem in your life.

00:02:46.581 --> 00:02:48.642
And then we had all sorts of processes.

00:02:48.642 --> 00:02:50.476
Some of the things I wrote about in the book

00:02:50.476 --> 00:02:51.891
are from that class.

00:02:51.891 --> 00:02:54.564
And, it kind of was really interesting,

00:02:54.564 --> 00:02:57.234
because what I hit upon was this whole idea

00:02:57.234 --> 00:03:01.037
that if you do something, you're never the same.

00:03:01.296 --> 00:03:02.854
It's kind of really interesting.

00:03:02.854 --> 00:03:05.282
And so people did projects, which, you know,

00:03:05.282 --> 00:03:06.886
they weren't academic projects.

00:03:06.886 --> 00:03:08.656
They were jumping out of airplanes,

00:03:08.656 --> 00:03:12.507
and riding motorcycles, and building stuff, and all that.

00:03:12.707 --> 00:03:16.156
And I noticed it changed people.

00:03:16.156 --> 00:03:17.969
It gave them a kind of impetus.

00:03:17.969 --> 00:03:21.684
And this whole idea of doing became a very important part

00:03:21.684 --> 00:03:23.710
of my life and my teaching.

00:03:23.727 --> 00:03:26.564
And it's, rather than talk about it, let's do it.

00:03:26.564 --> 00:03:28.555
And the whole pragmatic thing is,

00:03:28.555 --> 00:03:30.052
if it works in your life, fine,

00:03:30.052 --> 00:03:33.174
it'll incorporate it and change you if you want to.

00:03:33.174 --> 00:03:35.578
If it doesn't work, forget about it and do something else.

00:03:35.656 --> 00:03:38.537
So, I kind of developed this class,

00:03:38.537 --> 00:03:40.391
which is called Designer in Society,

00:03:40.391 --> 00:03:44.435
based on these principles, and my life was pretty ok.

00:03:44.435 --> 00:03:46.914
And I developed something called a Creativity Workshop,

00:03:46.914 --> 00:03:49.555
which we give around the world, and for professors,

00:03:49.555 --> 00:03:50.962
and that was kind of great.

00:03:51.063 --> 00:03:54.490
And then about 12 years ago or so,

00:03:54.760 --> 00:03:58.038
this thing called the d.school appeared in my life,

00:03:58.038 --> 00:04:01.417
and it was sort of a sidelight for me.

00:04:01.530 --> 00:04:04.629
And then David Kelley, who was sort of the principal

00:04:04.749 --> 00:04:08.269
of the people who founded it, got cancer,

00:04:08.269 --> 00:04:10.965
and I was at home minding my own business

00:04:10.965 --> 00:04:13.820
Friday night at about 11 o'clock at night,

00:04:13.820 --> 00:04:15.602
and I got a call from Jim Plummer,

00:04:15.602 --> 00:04:18.057
who was the Dean of Engineering in those days.

00:04:18.057 --> 00:04:19.957
And he said, "I'm really uncomfortable

00:04:20.237 --> 00:04:22.833
"with David being out for his treatments,

00:04:22.833 --> 00:04:25.526
"not having a faculty person in charge there,"

00:04:25.526 --> 00:04:28.056
and "Would you do that till he comes back?"

00:04:28.056 --> 00:04:29.231
So, I said "Sure."

00:04:29.231 --> 00:04:31.466
I didn't negotiate or anything. I just said "Fine."

00:04:31.466 --> 00:04:34.354
And then Monday morning I walked into the d.school,

00:04:34.354 --> 00:04:36.100
and I never left. (chuckles)

00:04:36.100 --> 00:04:38.509
And it totally changed my life.

00:04:38.509 --> 00:04:41.258
And what it did is it kind of took the things

00:04:41.258 --> 00:04:43.424
we had been talking about always.

00:04:44.274 --> 00:04:45.820
In those days, we called it need-finding,

00:04:45.820 --> 00:04:48.302
and we changed it to human-centered design,

00:04:48.302 --> 00:04:50.783
and it kind of was a way to express all these things

00:04:50.783 --> 00:04:52.076
which were kind of,

00:04:54.108 --> 00:04:55.911
They were kind of part of my life,

00:04:55.911 --> 00:04:58.848
but really there was too many other pieces

00:04:58.848 --> 00:05:00.763
to make it all come together.

00:05:00.763 --> 00:05:02.216
And when I got to the d.school,

00:05:02.216 --> 00:05:05.618
it kind of crystallized this thing we call design thinking,

00:05:05.618 --> 00:05:08.652
is really what we've been talking about all along.

00:05:08.652 --> 00:05:10.533
And we really can use it for other things

00:05:10.533 --> 00:05:12.497
rather than designing stuff.

00:05:12.497 --> 00:05:14.195
So let me just make that clear.

00:05:14.195 --> 00:05:16.301
There's this thing called design thinking in the world,

00:05:16.301 --> 00:05:17.935
which you've probably heard about.

00:05:17.935 --> 00:05:20.560
And the way the name came about,

00:05:20.560 --> 00:05:23.178
it uses methods that traditionally were used

00:05:23.178 --> 00:05:25.736
by designers to design stuff,

00:05:26.002 --> 00:05:27.796
uses some of those ideas.

00:05:28.484 --> 00:05:30.890
But now it applies it to almost everything.

00:05:30.890 --> 00:05:34.718
So it's very widespread in business now, it's in health.

00:05:34.718 --> 00:05:37.826
I cannot tell you how many different groups want me

00:05:37.826 --> 00:05:39.825
to talk about design thinking.

00:05:39.894 --> 00:05:43.949
Nurses and trainers and all this kind of, you name it.

00:05:44.229 --> 00:05:46.059
Groups of people working with,

00:05:46.059 --> 00:05:49.000
want to somehow use design thinking in the world.

00:05:49.096 --> 00:05:50.627
And people come into the d.school

00:05:50.627 --> 00:05:52.935
and think we can cure cancer and all that.

00:05:53.056 --> 00:05:56.125
So, it kind of gets a little inflated.

00:05:56.125 --> 00:05:57.912
But, really, it's a kind of useful tool

00:05:57.912 --> 00:05:59.833
in problem-solving and all that.

00:05:59.926 --> 00:06:04.546
And what I decided to do is to apply it to ourselves.

00:06:04.764 --> 00:06:07.122
And I thought my colleagues would kill me,

00:06:07.122 --> 00:06:09.193
because one of the things in design thinking is,

00:06:09.193 --> 00:06:12.574
don't design for yourself, design for someone else, ok.

00:06:12.574 --> 00:06:14.144
And that's a really good thing,

00:06:14.144 --> 00:06:16.369
because others can see things you can't see.

00:06:16.683 --> 00:06:19.370
But to me, it doesn't mean you can't also use it,

00:06:19.580 --> 00:06:21.618
until something better comes along,

00:06:21.618 --> 00:06:22.876
use it on yourself.

00:06:23.090 --> 00:06:26.786
And so I tried to use the principles of design thinking,

00:06:26.786 --> 00:06:28.428
and my ideas in creativity,

00:06:28.428 --> 00:06:31.002
and my teaching from Designer in Society

00:06:31.002 --> 00:06:34.621
to make a kind of holistic thing for you or me

00:06:34.621 --> 00:06:36.200
to go forward in the world.

00:06:36.271 --> 00:06:38.410
And these are things I've used with students

00:06:38.410 --> 00:06:40.650
for, you know, 40-some odd years,

00:06:40.650 --> 00:06:41.995
so I know they work.

00:06:41.995 --> 00:06:43.317
And I've used them in my life,

00:06:43.317 --> 00:06:45.174
so I know that they work also.

00:06:45.174 --> 00:06:46.550
So what I want to do today

00:06:46.550 --> 00:06:48.706
is share some of these things with you.

00:06:48.940 --> 00:06:53.367
And the first thing is this whole idea of problem-solving.

00:06:53.637 --> 00:06:57.753
So, first of all, problems have a bad rap.

00:06:58.014 --> 00:07:00.448
Not around here, because Tina's brainwashed everyone

00:07:00.448 --> 00:07:01.790
that they're opportunities.

00:07:01.790 --> 00:07:05.389
But, basically, in the real world, problems have a bad rap,

00:07:05.389 --> 00:07:06.961
because people think, "Oh, it's a problem.

00:07:06.961 --> 00:07:09.895
"It's terrible," and all that stuff.

00:07:09.895 --> 00:07:11.972
But in fact, it's important to understand

00:07:11.972 --> 00:07:15.700
that that's where my life force comes from.

00:07:15.783 --> 00:07:17.274
And I'm sure there are others in the room

00:07:17.274 --> 00:07:19.813
whose life force comes from that also.

00:07:19.813 --> 00:07:22.280
So when I have a problem, I'm excited.

00:07:22.280 --> 00:07:23.714
I can't sleep. I get up.

00:07:23.714 --> 00:07:25.932
I'm always activated to do that.

00:07:25.932 --> 00:07:28.786
And in fact, that's kind of what it's about.

00:07:28.786 --> 00:07:31.444
And one of my favorite stories is a book

00:07:31.954 --> 00:07:36.940
that was written in the 1800s in imperial Russia

00:07:37.260 --> 00:07:39.799
about a character called Oblomov,

00:07:39.799 --> 00:07:42.981
and for the first 250 pages of the book,

00:07:42.981 --> 00:07:44.608
he doesn't get out of bed.

00:07:45.119 --> 00:07:46.929
And the whole idea, it's a fantasy,

00:07:46.929 --> 00:07:50.159
but the whole idea was to be critical of this aristocracy

00:07:50.159 --> 00:07:51.560
that it's no reason to get out of bed.

00:07:51.560 --> 00:07:53.336
Everything is taken care of for him.

00:07:53.336 --> 00:07:54.569
Why would you get out of bed

00:07:54.569 --> 00:07:57.016
if everything is taken care of for you?

00:07:57.016 --> 00:08:00.090
So that's the kind of idea to understand about problems.

00:08:00.090 --> 00:08:02.912
It's a good reason to get out of bed, ok.

00:08:02.912 --> 00:08:05.786
And if people get depressed, they don't get out of bed.

00:08:05.786 --> 00:08:08.388
So it's a good sign you want to get out of bed.

00:08:08.388 --> 00:08:10.323
So you get out of bed and that's great.

00:08:10.323 --> 00:08:12.103
And you got these things which are energizing,

00:08:12.103 --> 00:08:13.476
and you want to work on them.

00:08:13.476 --> 00:08:14.977
And we have them in our lives.

00:08:14.977 --> 00:08:18.740
And most of people in this room are great problem solvers.

00:08:18.874 --> 00:08:21.685
And I think of myself as a great problem solver.

00:08:21.685 --> 00:08:25.518
So the question is, why do people like you and me

00:08:25.518 --> 00:08:28.231
have things in our lives which are troubling,

00:08:28.231 --> 00:08:30.169
which we cannot handle?

00:08:31.047 --> 00:08:32.946
How come? We're so smart.

00:08:32.946 --> 00:08:34.057
You're a very smart guy.

00:08:34.057 --> 00:08:35.814
I can tell right off the bat.

00:08:35.814 --> 00:08:36.939
You're a smart guy too.

00:08:36.939 --> 00:08:38.628
And you're a smart woman, right?

00:08:38.628 --> 00:08:40.786
Sure, we all know that. (chuckling)

00:08:41.406 --> 00:08:43.377
I must say, this is an aside,

00:08:43.377 --> 00:08:47.218
but I went to a memorial service for Sandy Dornbusch,

00:08:47.218 --> 00:08:50.527
who was a colleague I knew for many years from Sociology.

00:08:50.527 --> 00:08:54.098
And I heard he would tell his students,

00:08:54.098 --> 00:08:55.875
"Don't worry that you don't feel smart.

00:08:55.875 --> 00:08:57.357
"When you go home for Christmas break,

00:08:57.357 --> 00:08:58.967
"you'll feel smart again."

00:08:58.967 --> 00:09:02.053
So, his point was, everyone is so smart around here,

00:09:02.053 --> 00:09:03.743
it's hard to feel smart, ok.

00:09:03.743 --> 00:09:07.901
So, whether that's true or not, it's a nice thought.

00:09:08.038 --> 00:09:12.119
So, we're here now, and the question is, you're ok.

00:09:12.206 --> 00:09:13.964
Most of you are dressed properly.

00:09:13.964 --> 00:09:15.228
You found this room.

00:09:15.228 --> 00:09:17.009
You kind of got into Stanford.

00:09:17.009 --> 00:09:19.051
You know, you have a lot of accomplishments.

00:09:19.051 --> 00:09:22.597
So why is it that you and I lose sleep about something?

00:09:23.588 --> 00:09:26.117
And I have a theory what that is.

00:09:26.322 --> 00:09:28.582
And I say that most of the time,

00:09:28.582 --> 00:09:31.690
it's because we're trying to solve the wrong problem.

00:09:31.846 --> 00:09:36.253
So my statement to you is, if you have a problem

00:09:36.253 --> 00:09:38.708
that you are really losing sleep over,

00:09:38.708 --> 00:09:40.347
and that you cannot solve,

00:09:40.347 --> 00:09:43.169
it's because you're working on the wrong problem.

00:09:43.531 --> 00:09:45.022
It's as simple as that.

00:09:45.022 --> 00:09:47.382
And I have seen that in my PhD,

00:09:47.902 --> 00:09:51.454
you know I used to be an expert on robotics and kinematics

00:09:51.454 --> 00:09:55.916
and stuff like that, and I would have PhD students come in,

00:09:55.916 --> 00:09:57.511
and I knew the field very well,

00:09:57.511 --> 00:09:59.143
I'd been researching for many years,

00:09:59.143 --> 00:10:00.593
and I'd give them a problem,

00:10:00.593 --> 00:10:02.628
and we'd work for a couple years,

00:10:03.128 --> 00:10:05.159
and then suddenly we'd realize,

00:10:05.159 --> 00:10:06.219
"Oh, it wasn't really,

00:10:06.219 --> 00:10:07.920
"The real question is this."

00:10:08.009 --> 00:10:10.578
It wasn't a big change, but we realized suddenly

00:10:10.578 --> 00:10:11.971
what the gem was.

00:10:11.971 --> 00:10:13.962
And we did that, the student could finish up

00:10:13.962 --> 00:10:17.307
in a couple of months, write up, and go on with their lives.

00:10:17.425 --> 00:10:19.167
And it was a really great example

00:10:19.167 --> 00:10:20.904
where here we had a lot of expertise

00:10:20.904 --> 00:10:23.649
and yet we didn't know what the real question was.

00:10:23.715 --> 00:10:26.154
And I've seen that when companies come in and they,

00:10:26.154 --> 00:10:29.129
or consulting things, they ask you to do something,

00:10:29.129 --> 00:10:30.624
and it's the wrong question.

00:10:30.624 --> 00:10:32.371
And it appears over and over again.

00:10:32.371 --> 00:10:35.267
And it certainly appears in my everyday life.

00:10:35.475 --> 00:10:39.613
So, what I want you to understand is, it's really easy,

00:10:39.613 --> 00:10:43.133
it's what we call in design thinking reframing the problem.

00:10:43.498 --> 00:10:45.927
And the secret is to reframe it.

00:10:46.238 --> 00:10:48.691
And the question is, "How do I do that?"

00:10:48.702 --> 00:10:51.215
And the answer is really, really simple.

00:10:52.102 --> 00:10:55.786
And the way I suggest you do it, is you ask yourself

00:10:55.786 --> 00:10:59.172
what it would do for you if you solved the problem.

00:11:00.050 --> 00:11:01.965
Ask yourself what it would do for you

00:11:01.965 --> 00:11:03.777
if you solved the problem.

00:11:09.147 --> 00:11:12.384
And that is the question you want to ask yourself.

00:11:16.214 --> 00:11:18.664
I'll give you an example where I totally failed.

00:11:20.116 --> 00:11:24.436
So I rented a car, and I drove up to the wine country.

00:11:24.513 --> 00:11:26.941
And eventually the car ran out of gas,

00:11:26.941 --> 00:11:29.036
and I pulled into the gas station.

00:11:29.036 --> 00:11:31.172
And I started to look for the release

00:11:31.172 --> 00:11:34.146
for the cover to the gas tank.

00:11:34.232 --> 00:11:36.058
And I must have looked for ten minutes,

00:11:36.058 --> 00:11:37.226
in the glove compartment,

00:11:37.226 --> 00:11:40.175
every place normal designers would put it,

00:11:40.175 --> 00:11:41.632
and I couldn't find it.

00:11:41.632 --> 00:11:43.573
I was kind of frustrated, and then I noticed

00:11:43.573 --> 00:11:46.233
a similar car drove up for gas.

00:11:46.233 --> 00:11:48.033
So I went up to the lady, and I said,

00:11:48.033 --> 00:11:49.984
"I know this is a stupid question,

00:11:49.984 --> 00:11:51.651
"but where is the release?"

00:11:51.651 --> 00:11:53.461
She said, "There is none."

00:11:54.503 --> 00:11:58.281
And so at that point, I realized what I was doing.

00:11:59.291 --> 00:12:02.272
I had framed the question to where the release is.

00:12:02.479 --> 00:12:04.880
And why would I, if I played my game,

00:12:04.880 --> 00:12:07.706
which I didn't of course, but if I played my game,

00:12:07.706 --> 00:12:09.108
I would say, "What would it do for me

00:12:09.108 --> 00:12:10.897
"if I could find the release?

00:12:10.897 --> 00:12:13.445
