00:22:18.250 --> 00:22:19.894
You're not gonna let go either.

00:22:19.894 --> 00:22:20.686
Another one.

00:22:20.686 --> 00:22:22.203
These people won't let go.

00:22:22.203 --> 00:22:24.038
So, you see, if you're not letting go,

00:22:24.038 --> 00:22:26.397
you really don't want to solve the problem.

00:22:26.504 --> 00:22:27.811
It's as simple as that.

00:22:27.811 --> 00:22:30.073
You want to make your clients wrong for not paying.

00:22:30.073 --> 00:22:31.963
I agree they're a bunch of son of a bitches

00:22:31.963 --> 00:22:33.164
that they don't pay you.

00:22:33.164 --> 00:22:34.224
But that's the point.

00:22:34.224 --> 00:22:35.487
You have to, if that's really,

00:22:35.577 --> 00:22:37.300
Now I know we're playing a game here,

00:22:37.300 --> 00:22:39.074
and I'm gonna give her a reward anyway.

00:22:39.074 --> 00:22:40.110
I'm not that tough.

00:22:40.110 --> 00:22:42.533
But, the point simply is,

00:22:42.993 --> 00:22:45.253
I think you can see in these examples,

00:22:45.901 --> 00:22:48.715
that, in fact, it is simply that

00:22:48.715 --> 00:22:50.474
if you're really stuck, if this is real,

00:22:50.474 --> 00:22:51.983
I mean this is just a game we're playing,

00:22:51.983 --> 00:22:54.144
but if this is really a problem you're stuck with,

00:22:54.144 --> 00:22:55.454
you have to be willing to say,

00:22:55.454 --> 00:22:58.214
"What would it really do for me if I got that?"

00:22:58.214 --> 00:22:59.997
Not, "How will I do it?"

00:22:59.997 --> 00:23:02.553
See, we're stuck in the, we're implementing a solution.

00:23:02.553 --> 00:23:04.439
We're not really looking at the question.

00:23:04.439 --> 00:23:06.802
And if her case, the question is how do I get money

00:23:06.802 --> 00:23:08.696
to do other things?

00:23:09.323 --> 00:23:10.991
It would be great if she got it from her client,

00:23:10.991 --> 00:23:12.860
but if she's not, so what?

00:23:13.456 --> 00:23:15.217
No, it's "so what?"

00:23:16.153 --> 00:23:17.609
There's other ways of getting money,

00:23:17.609 --> 00:23:18.997
if that's what the question is,

00:23:18.997 --> 00:23:21.558
rather than in bunking herself and not doing it.

00:23:21.558 --> 00:23:23.205
I hope she gets it from your client,

00:23:23.205 --> 00:23:25.305
but, if you don't, you don't want to spend your life

00:23:25.305 --> 00:23:26.786
worrying about that.

00:23:26.786 --> 00:23:28.149
You want to go on and do the other thing,

00:23:28.149 --> 00:23:29.461
and this is the way you do it.

00:23:29.461 --> 00:23:31.002
Say, "What do I really want?"

00:23:31.002 --> 00:23:33.918
And the solution space opens up tremendously.

00:23:33.998 --> 00:23:35.685
You could also use the gun and the robbery

00:23:35.685 --> 00:23:37.434
that I suggested for her.

00:23:37.434 --> 00:23:38.124
It works for a while.

00:23:38.124 --> 00:23:38.972
Ok, good.

00:23:39.096 --> 00:23:41.169
Any questions about the process? Yeah?

00:23:41.578 --> 00:23:42.556
- [Voiceover] I'm curious how,

00:23:42.556 --> 00:23:44.353
when do you know that you need--

00:23:44.353 --> 00:23:46.142
- I have to get closer.

00:23:46.142 --> 00:23:47.453
- [Voiceover] Sorry, when do you know

00:23:47.453 --> 00:23:48.987
that you need to give up?

00:23:48.987 --> 00:23:50.625
- When do you know when you need to give up?

00:23:50.625 --> 00:23:51.978
We'll get to that later on,

00:23:51.978 --> 00:23:53.804
but it's whenever you want to.

00:23:53.804 --> 00:23:55.538
There's no such thing as "have to give up".

00:23:55.538 --> 00:23:57.550
And there's no such thing as "have to do it".

00:23:57.550 --> 00:23:59.465
It's up to you, you know what I mean?

00:23:59.465 --> 00:24:02.535
All I'm saying is if you're stuck and you're losing sleep,

00:24:02.535 --> 00:24:05.051
the chances are it's the wrong question.

00:24:05.180 --> 00:24:06.343
Or else you would have found it.

00:24:06.343 --> 00:24:08.592
She says she's working on it for three months.

00:24:08.592 --> 00:24:10.726
If she's been really working on it for three months,

00:24:10.726 --> 00:24:11.960
she's not gonna find it

00:24:11.960 --> 00:24:13.907
if she works on it for another three months.

00:24:13.907 --> 00:24:16.236
She has to find the real question,

00:24:16.236 --> 00:24:18.780
and then the answer will be obvious.

00:24:18.780 --> 00:24:20.465
It will be obvious.

00:24:20.630 --> 00:24:21.306
It's a simple,

00:24:21.306 --> 00:24:23.044
I've been through it a lot of times.

00:24:23.204 --> 00:24:24.445
And I'll give you my book.

00:24:24.445 --> 00:24:26.543
I have some examples how I really screwed up.

00:24:26.543 --> 00:24:27.888
So I learned the hard way.

00:24:27.888 --> 00:24:28.825
Ok? Good.

00:24:28.825 --> 00:24:31.040
Ok, so that's the first thing.

00:24:31.040 --> 00:24:32.039
If you're gonna solve problems,

00:24:32.039 --> 00:24:34.760
you really want to be working on the right problem.

00:24:35.620 --> 00:24:37.229
And my good example,

00:24:37.229 --> 00:24:38.384
Yeah, go ahead.

00:24:38.384 --> 00:24:39.975
- I was asking about the process.

00:24:39.975 --> 00:24:42.784
What if you want to learn how to solve

00:24:42.784 --> 00:24:44.976
a particular type of problem?

00:24:44.976 --> 00:24:48.605
- Yes. That's too hypothetical for me, ok.

00:24:48.774 --> 00:24:49.429
- Well, like in--

00:24:49.429 --> 00:24:51.663
- Let me show you what it's about.

00:24:51.831 --> 00:24:54.594
This is my favorite story in this issue.

00:24:55.046 --> 00:24:57.222
There's a drunk walking along.

00:24:57.323 --> 00:24:58.965
And he hits a lamppost.

00:24:59.415 --> 00:25:01.364
And he bounces off.

00:25:01.550 --> 00:25:03.302
And he hits a lamppost.

00:25:03.552 --> 00:25:05.155
And he bounces off.

00:25:05.155 --> 00:25:07.346
And we can give the lamppost various names.

00:25:07.346 --> 00:25:09.959
But it's a lamppost, and he bounces off.

00:25:10.125 --> 00:25:11.007
And he goes back.

00:25:11.007 --> 00:25:11.912
We don't have that much time.

00:25:11.912 --> 00:25:13.437
He hits the lamppost, and he goes,

00:25:13.437 --> 00:25:15.746
"I give up. They got me surrounded."

00:25:15.746 --> 00:25:17.519
(audience laughs)

00:25:17.519 --> 00:25:19.027
And that's the way you are.

00:25:19.027 --> 00:25:20.335
That's exactly the way you are,

00:25:20.335 --> 00:25:22.021
but you're not drunk.

00:25:22.081 --> 00:25:23.486
If you're not willing to let go,

00:25:23.486 --> 00:25:25.451
you're gonna keep banging against it.

00:25:25.451 --> 00:25:26.948
You're gonna keep banging against it,

00:25:26.948 --> 00:25:28.586
and then you're gonna surrender

00:25:28.586 --> 00:25:30.758
because they have you surrounded.

00:25:30.758 --> 00:25:33.315
And if you're not drunk, you go, "Oh, an obstacle,

00:25:33.315 --> 00:25:35.819
an obstacle, an obstacle, an obstacle," and

00:25:35.819 --> 00:25:38.427
"Oh, that's it. I got it. I'm through."

00:25:38.962 --> 00:25:39.846
You understand?

00:25:39.846 --> 00:25:42.224
The whole idea is, everyone's gonna have obstacles

00:25:42.224 --> 00:25:44.174
in their lives if it's a tough problem,

00:25:44.174 --> 00:25:45.387
or else you solved it.

00:25:45.387 --> 00:25:46.227
You're dressed properly.

00:25:46.227 --> 00:25:47.074
You got to Stanford.

00:25:47.074 --> 00:25:48.772
You got a front-row seat here.

00:25:48.772 --> 00:25:50.520
You solved a lot of problems.

00:25:50.520 --> 00:25:51.875
You just got this one little thing.

00:25:51.875 --> 00:25:53.855
Your clients are not paying you.

00:25:54.795 --> 00:25:55.743
So what?

00:25:56.411 --> 00:25:57.561
You're gonna make your whole life

00:25:57.561 --> 00:25:59.214
your clients aren't paying you?

00:25:59.214 --> 00:26:01.340
Or are you gonna go ahead and do what you want to do?

00:26:02.518 --> 00:26:03.491
All right, good.

00:26:03.826 --> 00:26:05.976
She's shaking, but it's still not there.

00:26:05.976 --> 00:26:08.034
Anyway, let me shut my phone off quick

00:26:08.034 --> 00:26:09.642
before I get embarrassed.

00:26:09.642 --> 00:26:12.438
Ok, so you don't want to be like the drunk

00:26:12.438 --> 00:26:13.981
even though you're sober.

00:26:13.981 --> 00:26:15.759
But most of us act that way.

00:26:15.769 --> 00:26:19.058
Really, it's just a matter of walking around the obstacle.

00:26:19.093 --> 00:26:22.888
So, what are ways that kind of get us entrapped?

00:26:23.118 --> 00:26:25.048
Assuming you have the right problem,

00:26:25.048 --> 00:26:26.995
it often solves itself.

00:26:28.156 --> 00:26:29.810
However, things happen.

00:26:29.810 --> 00:26:31.260
Things come up.

00:26:31.260 --> 00:26:33.593
One of the first things that come up is,

00:26:33.963 --> 00:26:35.347
it doesn't kind of work.

00:26:35.347 --> 00:26:38.862
Like, he throws his clock across the room,

00:26:38.862 --> 00:26:41.435
but somehow he doesn't get up and get it.

00:26:41.435 --> 00:26:43.786
So, if I asked him why he doesn't go up and get it,

00:26:43.786 --> 00:26:45.293
he'll give me a reason.

00:26:45.293 --> 00:26:46.461
He's a very reasonable guy.

00:26:46.461 --> 00:26:48.038
I can tell, he's a reasonable guy.

00:26:48.038 --> 00:26:49.809
He'll give me a reason why he doesn't get up

00:26:49.809 --> 00:26:50.993
to get a clock.

00:26:50.993 --> 00:26:53.555
If I ask her something, she'll give me a reason

00:26:53.555 --> 00:26:56.456
why she can't change social behavior.

00:26:56.456 --> 00:26:59.032
If I ask her something, she'll give me a reason

00:26:59.032 --> 00:27:02.118
why she can't collect from her clients.

00:27:02.118 --> 00:27:03.764
And they're all reasons.

00:27:03.884 --> 00:27:08.205
Except in my world, and I hate to use this bad word here,

00:27:08.205 --> 00:27:10.208
all reasons are bullshit.

00:27:10.208 --> 00:27:12.802
All reasons for human or personal behavior

00:27:12.802 --> 00:27:14.207
are just bullshit.

00:27:14.207 --> 00:27:17.043
And I mean it in the sense of,

00:27:17.043 --> 00:27:18.585
you don't know what it is.

00:27:18.585 --> 00:27:19.694
You're making it up.

00:27:19.694 --> 00:27:21.239
So I use "bullshit" in the sense of,

00:27:21.239 --> 00:27:22.330
you're making up something

00:27:22.330 --> 00:27:24.970
which you don't know if it's true or not.

00:27:24.979 --> 00:27:26.633
And it goes like this.

00:27:26.633 --> 00:27:30.844
If I ask you for the reason you did something,

00:27:30.844 --> 00:27:33.203
if you ask me why I'm here,

00:27:33.203 --> 00:27:35.648
I'll tell you, "Tina invited me."

00:27:36.323 --> 00:27:38.300
That is not, there is no lie in that.

00:27:38.300 --> 00:27:40.054
But it's bullshit.

00:27:40.224 --> 00:27:41.717
'Cause Tina could've invited me,

00:27:41.717 --> 00:27:43.324
and I could have not come.

00:27:43.888 --> 00:27:46.259
And I could've probably, if I really wanted to get here,

00:27:46.259 --> 00:27:50.032
maybe gotten past Tina and got someone else to invite me.

00:27:50.112 --> 00:27:51.407
So, the point simply is,

00:27:51.407 --> 00:27:55.278
there's a million reasons I got here,

00:27:55.326 --> 00:27:57.631
one of which is, Tina invited me.

00:27:57.631 --> 00:27:58.319
And if you ask me,

00:27:58.319 --> 00:28:00.283
that's the most reasonable thing to tell you.

00:28:00.283 --> 00:28:01.549
So I want to be a reasonable guy.

00:28:01.549 --> 00:28:03.605
I'll tell you, and you'll go about your business.

00:28:03.605 --> 00:28:05.498
And that's fine. Who cares?

00:28:05.498 --> 00:28:07.451
The problem is that, often,

00:28:07.689 --> 00:28:11.804
we hide excuses as reasons,

00:28:11.804 --> 00:28:13.293
and we'll never change our behavior

00:28:13.293 --> 00:28:15.305
if we don't tell ourselves the truth.

00:28:15.611 --> 00:28:17.844
And I'll elaborate on that in a minute.

00:28:17.844 --> 00:28:21.026
So, the point simply is, there's a lot of things that go on.

00:28:21.026 --> 00:28:23.363
Tina and I have a very complicated relationship,

00:28:23.363 --> 00:28:24.991
starting from when we first met,

00:28:24.991 --> 00:28:27.669
where we hated each other and all of that.

00:28:27.857 --> 00:28:30.121
And now, she's the most supportive friend I have

00:28:30.121 --> 00:28:32.351
in terms of my book and stuff like that.

00:28:32.351 --> 00:28:34.201
So, I said, there's like a lot of things

00:28:34.201 --> 00:28:35.999
going on between me and Tina.

00:28:35.999 --> 00:28:38.529
And between anyone, any friends, you know what I mean?

00:28:38.529 --> 00:28:40.357
Nothing secret's going on.

00:28:40.357 --> 00:28:41.530
(audience laughs)

00:28:41.530 --> 00:28:43.312
Came out the wrong way.

00:28:43.312 --> 00:28:45.399
Mike, I didn't mean it, Mike.

00:28:46.649 --> 00:28:47.830
It came out the wrong way.

00:28:47.830 --> 00:28:50.067
But, you know, we have a complicated relationship

00:28:50.067 --> 00:28:51.989
as we do with all our friends.

00:28:52.159 --> 00:28:55.014
So, the fact that you choose to give your reason,

00:28:55.014 --> 00:28:56.652
you're just singling something out.

00:28:56.652 --> 00:28:59.931
My favorite is, right now my mouth is terribly dry.

00:29:00.402 --> 00:29:02.771
So if you ask me why my mouth is dry,

00:29:02.771 --> 00:29:05.342
I'll say I'm talking a lot.

00:29:05.489 --> 00:29:07.011
And that seems obvious.

00:29:07.011 --> 00:29:09.712
However, every time I talk my mouth is dry.

00:29:09.712 --> 00:29:11.366
However, my wife is always telling me,

00:29:11.366 --> 00:29:12.345
"Drink more water."

00:29:12.345 --> 00:29:14.178
I'm chronically dehydrated.

00:29:14.338 --> 00:29:17.244
When I go to Burning Man, I don't pee the right colors.

00:29:17.244 --> 00:29:19.085
(audience laughs)

00:29:19.085 --> 00:29:21.487
My whole life is one of dehydration.

00:29:21.487 --> 00:29:23.973
Every time I talk, I know this is gonna come up.

00:29:23.973 --> 00:29:27.666
I also, of course, drank a bottle of wine last night.

00:29:27.666 --> 00:29:29.306
Et cetera, et cetera, et cetera.

00:29:29.306 --> 00:29:30.694
So there's a lot of things

00:29:30.694 --> 00:29:35.322
that could have caused my dehydrated, dry mouth.

00:29:35.344 --> 00:29:38.137
So the one I tell you is, I'm talking a lot.

00:29:38.229 --> 00:29:39.575
It's obvious, it's a way,

00:29:39.765 --> 00:29:41.309
that's the way you have to be in life.

00:29:41.309 --> 00:29:43.473
But I will never change my behavior

00:29:43.473 --> 00:29:48.366
if I don't tell myself that I really don't know the reason.

00:29:48.916 --> 00:29:51.599
Now let me explain where my epiphany comes from.

00:29:51.599 --> 00:29:55.983
My epiphany comes from a board meeting in Berkeley.

00:29:55.983 --> 00:29:57.152
I was on a start-up company,

00:29:57.152 --> 00:29:58.810
I was on the board of directors.

00:29:58.810 --> 00:30:00.060
And we'd have regular meetings.

00:30:00.060 --> 00:30:03.553
And the company didn't fail but did quite well,

00:30:03.553 --> 00:30:05.160
so I had to keep going to meetings.

00:30:05.160 --> 00:30:07.308
And I was always late.

00:30:07.308 --> 00:30:09.719
I was always late, always.

00:30:09.719 --> 00:30:12.522
And I'd come in, and those days it was Highway 17.

00:30:12.522 --> 00:30:13.689
And I'd come in, I'd say,

00:30:13.689 --> 00:30:16.878
"Oh the traffic on Highway 17 was terrible."

00:30:16.878 --> 00:30:19.119
And they'd all say, "It's ok Bernie.

00:30:19.119 --> 00:30:21.534
"We're just glad nothing happened" and all that,

00:30:21.534 --> 00:30:22.592
and "It's ok."

00:30:22.902 --> 00:30:24.453
And I knew it wasn't ok.

00:30:24.453 --> 00:30:26.985
I mean these were people who have lives and all that,

00:30:26.985 --> 00:30:29.693
and I was just holding them back.

00:30:29.693 --> 00:30:32.923
And of course I realized what it was is,

00:30:32.923 --> 00:30:34.979
I wasn't leaving enough time.

00:30:34.979 --> 00:30:37.021
And I wasn't leaving enough time,

00:30:37.021 --> 00:30:40.273
since I didn't give them enough valence in my life.

00:30:40.273 --> 00:30:42.533
It wasn't important enough to me.

00:30:42.658 --> 00:30:45.031
So I realized I ought to make a decision.

00:30:45.031 --> 00:30:47.150
I either resign from the board,

00:30:47.150 --> 00:30:49.461
or I ought to be there on time.

00:30:49.772 --> 00:30:53.249
And being on time was as easy as getting out of bed.

00:30:53.328 --> 00:30:55.593
And it was just simply not doing,

00:30:55.593 --> 00:30:58.816
in those days we had a teletype in my office to do email,

00:30:58.816 --> 00:31:01.911
not doing a few extra emails, not making phone calls,

00:31:01.911 --> 00:31:04.182
no cell phones in those days believe it or not,

00:31:04.182 --> 00:31:06.485
not making a few extra phone calls,

00:31:06.485 --> 00:31:08.481
and not talking to a colleague in the hall

00:31:08.481 --> 00:31:10.191
as I was leaving the building,

00:31:10.191 --> 00:31:13.426
and leaving enough time so even if there was,

00:31:13.426 --> 00:31:16.640
imagine that, traffic in Palo Alto and traffic in Berkeley

00:31:16.640 --> 00:31:19.941
and traffic on Highway 17, I'd get there on time.

00:31:20.296 --> 00:31:23.839
Once I got that epiphany, it changed my whole life.

00:31:24.007 --> 00:31:27.049
I used to be the person who was always late to everything.

00:31:27.096 --> 00:31:30.332
Now I'm the pain in the ass who starts everything on time.

00:31:30.792 --> 00:31:32.446
I'm always on time.

00:31:32.643 --> 00:31:34.903
My former chairman, he was terrified about me,

00:31:34.903 --> 00:31:36.066
'cause he was always late.

00:31:36.066 --> 00:31:38.998
"Bernie's coming. I gotta be on time, I gotta be on time."

00:31:38.998 --> 00:31:40.282
And people who read the book,

00:31:40.282 --> 00:31:42.310
they're afraid to come in two minutes late.

00:31:42.310 --> 00:31:43.709
So it's not that really bad,

00:31:43.709 --> 00:31:46.019
but it's a great example of reasons.

00:31:46.019 --> 00:31:48.202
So, the being late was bullshit.

00:31:48.202 --> 00:31:50.371
There was traffic on Highway 17.

00:31:50.371 --> 00:31:52.573
But that was not the reason I was late.

00:31:52.573 --> 00:31:54.441
There were a million other reasons I was late.

00:31:54.621 --> 00:31:57.225
So the point simply is, who cares?

00:31:58.593 --> 00:32:01.187
You care if you want to change your behavior.

00:32:01.270 --> 00:32:03.662
Just like my epiphany about,

00:32:03.662 --> 00:32:07.503
it wasn't the traffic on Highway 17 that was making me late,

00:32:07.503 --> 00:32:10.010
changed my behavior about time.

00:32:10.010 --> 00:32:11.474
It can change your behavior

00:32:11.474 --> 00:32:13.443
about a lot of things if you care.

00:32:13.443 --> 00:32:14.628
And the way you have to do it,

00:32:14.628 --> 00:32:17.394
is not to tell yourself a lie.

00:32:17.394 --> 00:32:18.957
And the way you have a lie,

00:32:18.957 --> 00:32:20.256
is you tell yourself a reason.

00:32:20.256 --> 00:32:23.025
Because you're so complex.

00:32:23.025 --> 00:32:25.408
You and I are such complex beings,

00:32:25.408 --> 00:32:28.690
that there's no one, single reason for anything.

00:32:30.061 --> 00:32:32.750
I always like to tell people, if some progenitor

00:32:32.750 --> 00:32:34.738
three generations ago hadn't had sex,

00:32:34.738 --> 00:32:36.123
you wouldn't have been here.

00:32:36.123 --> 00:32:38.575
So that's why my mouth's dry, you know.

00:32:38.755 --> 00:32:39.950
It's nonsense, right?

00:32:39.969 --> 00:32:41.455
So that's stupid, isn't it?

00:32:41.455 --> 00:32:42.953
So the whole idea is simply,

00:32:42.953 --> 00:32:44.877
you're picking out the one that makes sense,

00:32:44.877 --> 00:32:46.214
but there's a million reasons.

00:32:46.214 --> 00:32:48.046
There's not a reason for anything.

00:32:48.046 --> 00:32:49.728
And the other interesting thing is

00:32:49.728 --> 00:32:51.476
that people have done experiments.

00:32:51.476 --> 00:32:53.583
They've put people in MRI machines,

00:32:53.583 --> 00:32:56.862
and they've had them push a button or something.

00:32:56.926 --> 00:32:59.775
And they look at the parts of the brain that fire,

00:32:59.775 --> 00:33:01.171
and then they ask them what they did.

00:33:01.171 --> 00:33:03.236
And it turns out, the part of the brain

00:33:03.236 --> 00:33:05.650
that works on the actual,

00:33:05.650 --> 00:33:08.730
mechanical, muscular control

00:33:08.730 --> 00:33:11.549
fires before the part of the brain, the cortex,

00:33:11.549 --> 00:33:13.362
that works on the reasons.

00:33:13.399 --> 00:33:17.268
So what happens in most cases is you do what you do,

00:33:17.268 --> 00:33:19.069
and then you make up a reason for it.

00:33:19.069 --> 00:33:20.961
And that's what a reasonable person is.

00:33:20.961 --> 00:33:23.197
A reasonable person can explain their behavior,

00:33:23.197 --> 00:33:24.237
and they're reasonable.

00:33:24.237 --> 00:33:26.019
So you want to be a reasonable person.

00:33:26.019 --> 00:33:28.308
However, you want to not fool yourself.

00:33:28.308 --> 00:33:31.107
So what I do, if you ask me why I came here,

00:33:31.107 --> 00:33:32.396
or why my mouth is dry,

00:33:32.396 --> 00:33:34.927
I'll tell you I'm talking a lot or Tina invited me.

00:33:34.927 --> 00:33:36.389
And I know that's bullshit.

00:33:36.389 --> 00:33:38.352
Now it doesn't matter in this case.

00:33:38.352 --> 00:33:40.464
However, in some cases, like coming late

00:33:40.464 --> 00:33:43.547
to the board meeting, like other things, it does matter.

00:33:43.547 --> 00:33:45.548
And I will never change my behavior

00:33:45.718 --> 00:33:47.969
as long as I tell myself that lie.

00:33:47.969 --> 00:33:50.665
So what I do if you ask me a question, I'm reasonable,

00:33:50.665 --> 00:33:52.103
I give you a reasonable answer.

00:33:52.103 --> 00:33:55.858
And I go, "I'm never gonna say that again."

00:33:55.858 --> 00:33:57.219
And of course I say it again.

00:33:57.219 --> 00:33:59.602
But eventually I change.

00:34:00.105 --> 00:34:02.849
So the point simply is, if you get the view

00:34:02.849 --> 00:34:06.638
that all social reasons for your behavior are really,

00:34:06.638 --> 00:34:08.400
you don't know what they are.

00:34:08.400 --> 00:34:09.779
And you're just making it up,

00:34:09.779 --> 00:34:11.293
you'll be much more powerful.

00:34:11.293 --> 00:34:13.038
And if your behavior is not what you want,

00:34:13.038 --> 00:34:14.131
you can change it.

00:34:14.131 --> 00:34:17.155
And don't use the reason 'cause that's just an excuse.

00:34:17.155 --> 00:34:19.583
And we all know excuses are pejorative things.

00:34:19.583 --> 00:34:21.377
So if I ask him why he doesn't get out of bed,

00:34:21.377 --> 00:34:23.943
he'll give me a million excuses why he can't get out of bed.

00:34:23.943 --> 00:34:25.948
But if he actually wants to get out of bed,

00:34:25.948 --> 00:34:28.133
and he stops giving himself the bullshit reasons,

00:34:28.133 --> 00:34:30.270
he could get out of bed, ok.

00:34:30.444 --> 00:34:31.917
And get less done.

00:34:31.917 --> 00:34:33.615
Or more done, I forget which you want to do, ok.

00:34:33.615 --> 00:34:35.233
(audience laughs)

00:34:35.233 --> 00:34:36.595
But at any rate.

00:34:37.215 --> 00:34:38.400
And it works so well.

00:34:38.400 --> 00:34:41.466
I get, and funny, it just happened this morning again.

00:34:41.626 --> 00:34:45.651
I get maybe four emails a week from some student

00:34:45.651 --> 00:34:48.282
somewhere in the world who wants to come to Stanford

00:34:48.282 --> 00:34:50.239
and do a PhD with me.

00:34:50.830 --> 00:34:52.340
I could ignore those emails.

00:34:52.340 --> 00:34:54.433
I don't have any connection to most of these people.

00:34:54.433 --> 00:34:55.393
It's not that someone,

00:34:55.393 --> 00:34:57.678
they're just picking me randomly.

00:34:57.678 --> 00:34:59.725
And they're probably going to every professor in the world.

00:34:59.725 --> 00:35:01.767
I somehow feel it's nice to respond.

00:35:01.767 --> 00:35:03.240
They put a lot of work into it.

00:35:03.240 --> 00:35:05.778
They give me their resume, their life story.

00:35:05.778 --> 00:35:07.196
So I used to say, "I'm sorry.

00:35:07.196 --> 00:35:10.193
"I can't take you because I don't have any money."

00:35:10.193 --> 00:35:11.634
Or "I'm going on sabbatical."

00:35:11.634 --> 00:35:14.457
Or something nice to let 'em down easy.

00:35:14.457 --> 00:35:16.108
And what would invariably happen is,

00:35:16.108 --> 00:35:17.484
I'd get pushback.

00:35:17.567 --> 00:35:18.683
"Oh, well you don't have any money?

00:35:18.683 --> 00:35:19.552
"I have a rich uncle.

00:35:19.552 --> 00:35:20.641
"He'll support me."

00:35:20.641 --> 00:35:21.581
Or "You're going on sabbatical?

00:35:21.581 --> 00:35:23.315
"How about I come a year from now?"

00:35:23.315 --> 00:35:25.528
Whatever I'd say, they'd have some,

00:35:25.528 --> 00:35:26.678
and the email would go on

00:35:26.678 --> 00:35:29.308
and sometimes I'd just truncate out at first station.

00:35:29.308 --> 00:35:32.661
Now, that I'm enlightened, I say,

00:35:32.661 --> 00:35:35.085
"Sorry. Can't help you. Good luck."

00:35:35.171 --> 00:35:36.705
You know what happens?

00:35:36.705 --> 00:35:39.276
Half the time I get back one message:

00:35:39.276 --> 00:35:43.052
"Thank you very much for answering my email, Professor."

00:35:43.969 --> 00:35:45.986
I feel good about that.

00:35:45.986 --> 00:35:48.122
I feel much better than not answering.

00:35:48.122 --> 00:35:49.482
And it's over.

00:35:49.682 --> 00:35:51.709
And they apparently feel good about it.

00:35:51.709 --> 00:35:53.077
I don't know, maybe they're building me up.

00:35:53.077 --> 00:35:54.582
They think they're gonna come back next year.

00:35:54.582 --> 00:35:55.263
I don't know.

00:35:55.263 --> 00:35:56.766
But it just seems like so much better.

00:35:56.766 --> 00:35:58.975
I just say what I can do and what I can't do

00:35:58.975 --> 00:36:00.706
without giving the reasons.

00:36:00.706 --> 00:36:03.111
So what I'm advocating is, live your life

00:36:03.111 --> 00:36:05.783
as reason-free as you possibly can.

00:36:06.028 --> 00:36:07.180
It really helps.

00:36:07.320 --> 00:36:08.536
It really helps.

00:36:08.536 --> 00:36:10.624
Now, a lot of people

00:36:11.004 --> 00:36:14.384
in my little domain of the d.school

00:36:14.384 --> 00:36:18.116
and in the Design Group of Stanford have taken my workshops.

00:36:18.508 --> 00:36:22.763
So when some poor professor in my Design Group,

00:36:22.763 --> 00:36:25.397
who likes to complain a lot, gets up

00:36:25.397 --> 00:36:28.166
and he says at the meeting, "Well, the reason

00:36:28.166 --> 00:36:31.422
"I can't do this is the dean said blah blah blah,"

00:36:31.422 --> 00:36:35.856
he's greeted by a chorus, "That's a goooood reason."

00:36:36.412 --> 00:36:37.772
Meaning it's bullshit.

00:36:38.523 --> 00:36:42.622
So, it's a little embarrassing, but in fact

00:36:42.622 --> 00:36:45.140
he gets the benefit of knowing that it's just,

00:36:45.140 --> 00:36:47.161
the dean is not stopping him.

00:36:47.349 --> 00:36:48.568
The dean is not, the dean's doing

00:36:48.568 --> 00:36:50.271
whatever the dean is doing,

00:36:50.271 --> 00:36:52.140
and he could do whatever he does.

00:36:52.140 --> 00:36:55.516
So I want you to understand, when I say "gooood reason",

00:36:55.516 --> 00:36:57.553
I spell it with five o's.

00:36:57.723 --> 00:37:00.568
So I mean goooood reason is not a good reason at all.

00:37:00.568 --> 00:37:02.203
You got the idea? Ok.

00:37:02.203 --> 00:37:05.203
So what I want to do is give you that experience,

00:37:05.203 --> 00:37:07.026
'cause you don't want to take my word for it.

00:37:07.026 --> 00:37:09.664
Some of you look like, what's this guy talking about?

00:37:09.914 --> 00:37:11.502
So what I want you to do is,

00:37:11.512 --> 00:37:14.618
as close as you can, get a partner next to you,

00:37:14.618 --> 00:37:16.962
and if you can't, go to the next row.

00:37:16.962 --> 00:37:18.845
I'll give you two minutes to get a partner.

00:37:18.845 --> 00:37:20.687
So everyone just get a partner you could work with.

00:37:20.687 --> 00:37:22.974
Hopefully, someone seated next to you.

00:37:23.264 --> 00:37:24.739
And if you don't have a partner, raise your hand.

00:37:24.739 --> 00:37:26.171
We'll get you a partner.

00:37:26.191 --> 00:37:30.992
(audience talking to each other)

00:37:32.420 --> 00:37:34.247
So what you and I are gonna demonstrate--

00:37:34.377 --> 00:37:35.604
a partner, raise your hand.

00:37:37.234 --> 00:37:38.551
Anyone not have a partner?

00:37:38.551 --> 00:37:39.968
All right, anyone willing to admit

00:37:39.968 --> 00:37:40.896
they don't have a partner?

00:37:40.896 --> 00:37:41.533
Ok, good.

00:37:41.673 --> 00:37:42.526
So the idea is this.

00:37:42.526 --> 00:37:45.205
Tina and I are gonna demonstrate the game to you.

00:37:45.205 --> 00:37:46.951
So I'm gonna say to Tina,

00:37:47.011 --> 00:37:50.860
"The reason my mouth is dry is I'm talking so much."

00:37:50.926 --> 00:37:52.104
- That's a good reason.

00:37:52.164 --> 00:37:54.807
- The reason I'm here is 'cause you invited me.

00:37:54.987 --> 00:37:56.000
- That's a good reason.

00:37:56.000 --> 00:37:57.215
- The reason I'm talking about this

00:37:57.215 --> 00:37:58.999
is a way to advertise my book.

00:37:58.999 --> 00:38:00.260
- That's a good reason. (audience laughs)

00:38:00.260 --> 00:38:02.502
- Ok, see, these are all things

00:38:02.502 --> 00:38:05.029
in my life that are all true, ok.

00:38:05.238 --> 00:38:07.487
Now she's gonna tell me at some point,

00:38:07.607 --> 00:38:08.906
and I'm just gonna keep going.

00:38:08.906 --> 00:38:10.308
So one of you is gonna keep going.

00:38:10.308 --> 00:38:12.757
At some point, I'm gonna say, "Change roles."

00:38:12.757 --> 00:38:14.799
At that point, she's gonna give me something

00:38:14.799 --> 00:38:16.692
that's in her life, and I'm gonna say,

00:38:16.692 --> 00:38:18.498
"That's a goooood reason."

00:38:18.498 --> 00:38:20.099
And every time she tells me something,

00:38:20.099 --> 00:38:22.145
I'm gonna acknowledge that's a gooood reason, ok.

00:38:22.145 --> 00:38:23.424
And just, don't judge it.

00:38:23.424 --> 00:38:25.159
Just see how that feels to you.

00:38:25.159 --> 00:38:27.568
And really try and disprove Bernie, you know.

00:38:27.668 --> 00:38:30.740
In math, one counterexample disproves everything.

00:38:30.817 --> 00:38:32.979
Give something that's really a good thing

00:38:33.119 --> 00:38:34.515
that is a real reason.

00:38:34.515 --> 00:38:36.446
And when you get back "That's a goooood reason,"

00:38:36.446 --> 00:38:38.577
think about it for a minute, ok.

00:38:38.727 --> 00:38:40.253
Everyone got the experiment?

00:38:40.253 --> 00:38:41.638
It's a real life experiment.

00:38:41.638 --> 00:38:42.638
So, please start.

00:38:42.638 --> 00:38:44.415
One partner starts giving things,

00:38:44.415 --> 00:38:46.112
and then the partner says it's a good reason.

00:38:46.152 --> 00:38:47.791
Then I'll tell you when to switch roles.

00:38:47.791 --> 00:38:48.722
Go ahead.

00:38:48.722 --> 00:38:53.404
(audience talking to each other)

00:38:53.404 --> 00:38:55.821
Ok, thank you, thank you.

00:39:00.742 --> 00:39:03.528
Ok, anyone have any comments, or questions,

00:39:03.528 --> 00:39:06.348
or good reasons, anything anyone?

00:39:09.439 --> 00:39:11.875
So let me just explain it to you another way.

00:39:11.875 --> 00:39:15.497
If a student comes into my class and she says,

00:39:15.707 --> 00:39:18.394
"I'm sorry Professor, I have a flat tire on my bike.

00:39:18.394 --> 00:39:20.219
"I'm sorry for being late."

00:39:21.021 --> 00:39:23.922
That sounds great and even if she had the flat tire,

00:39:23.922 --> 00:39:26.678
if the rule was if you come into a class late,

00:39:26.678 --> 00:39:30.382
you fail the class, she would not have been late.

00:39:30.382 --> 00:39:32.113
Even if she had a flat tire.

00:39:32.740 --> 00:39:34.137
It's as simple as that.

00:39:34.137 --> 00:39:36.718
If the rule is, if you're late to a class,

00:39:36.718 --> 00:39:38.070
you get kicked out of Stanford,

00:39:38.070 --> 00:39:40.380
everything would start on time at Stanford.

00:39:41.469 --> 00:39:43.051
It's just a matter of caring.

00:39:43.051 --> 00:39:45.635
It's a matter of putting enough valence in it.

00:39:45.722 --> 00:39:47.124
I gave a talk at Google.

00:39:47.124 --> 00:39:47.911
This was a riot.

00:39:47.911 --> 00:39:50.050
This was like, it couldn't be tailor-made.

00:39:50.450 --> 00:39:52.641
There was one person who didn't have a partner,

00:39:52.641 --> 00:39:54.776
so I ended up working with her.

00:39:54.936 --> 00:39:58.242
So she says, and she came in late, so she says,

00:39:58.242 --> 00:40:02.015
"The reason I was late is I work in the other building."

00:40:02.015 --> 00:40:03.095
(audience giggles)

00:40:03.095 --> 00:40:03.598
I loved it.

00:40:03.598 --> 00:40:05.505
It was like I could have kissed her, right.

00:40:05.895 --> 00:40:08.699
The reason she's late is she works in the other building.

00:40:10.970 --> 00:40:12.711
And she thought that was real.

00:40:12.711 --> 00:40:15.715
(audience laughs)

00:40:15.715 --> 00:40:18.066
I was at a PhD exam in the Ed School,

00:40:18.386 --> 00:40:22.332
and we're waiting a half hour for a professor from Berkeley.

00:40:22.478 --> 00:40:23.531
And they're all sitting there saying,

00:40:23.531 --> 00:40:25.541
"Oh she's coming from Berkeley."

00:40:25.541 --> 00:40:26.775
So?

00:40:27.178 --> 00:40:29.050
She comes a half hour late, 'cause she's coming,

00:40:29.360 --> 00:40:32.311
so it's, the whole world doesn't care about that stuff.

00:40:32.311 --> 00:40:34.228
If you don't care, it's fine.

00:40:34.228 --> 00:40:35.883
If you don't care, it's fine.

00:40:35.915 --> 00:40:37.432
But you have to understand

00:40:37.432 --> 00:40:39.407
that if you want to change your behavior,

00:40:39.407 --> 00:40:42.142
you cannot use these excuses,

00:40:42.142 --> 00:40:43.948
which you call reasons.

00:40:43.948 --> 00:40:46.865
And you have to just give things more valence in your life.

00:40:46.947 --> 00:40:48.817
It's as simple as that, ok.

00:40:49.080 --> 00:40:51.265
And I won't even tell you about the collection mafia

00:40:51.265 --> 00:40:53.794
she's gonna hire to get the money.

00:40:53.944 --> 00:40:56.185
You have to just give it a little more valence.

00:40:56.185 --> 00:40:58.366
There are ways, ok, good.

00:40:58.366 --> 00:41:00.608
So, so far we have covered

00:41:00.608 --> 00:41:02.684
getting the right problem to work on,

00:41:02.684 --> 00:41:04.093
and we have covered the idea

00:41:04.093 --> 00:41:06.665
that reasons are bullshit in my world,

00:41:06.665 --> 00:41:09.775
and I hope you'll make them in your world, and

00:41:10.375 --> 00:41:11.570
Yes, Sir?

00:41:11.570 --> 00:41:12.435
- [Voiceover] So is it a matter

00:41:12.435 --> 00:41:14.955
of not telling ourselves the bullshit?

00:41:14.955 --> 00:41:17.395
- Yeah, yeah. Thank you, thank you.

00:41:17.395 --> 00:41:18.634
I'm gonna give you a present.

00:41:18.634 --> 00:41:20.546
I'm gonna give you two presents,

00:41:20.546 --> 00:41:24.324
because this is something you do not want to try at home.

00:41:24.727 --> 00:41:26.092
Believe me.

00:41:26.215 --> 00:41:28.564
I've been doing this for a long time.

00:41:28.647 --> 00:41:31.857
I have never once told my wife her reasons are bullshit.

00:41:31.862 --> 00:41:33.673
(audience laughs)

00:41:33.673 --> 00:41:37.066
I have never once told my sons their reasons are bullshit.

00:41:37.352 --> 00:41:40.661
I have never once told my buddies, my biking buddies,

00:41:40.661 --> 00:41:43.174
and I have never once told my colleagues in the d.school

00:41:43.174 --> 00:41:46.257
or in the Design Group that their reasons are bullshit.

00:41:46.257 --> 00:41:48.344
I just tell Bernie Roth.

00:41:48.579 --> 00:41:50.919
If I fix myself, the world is better.

00:41:51.246 --> 00:41:52.960
You cannot do it.

00:41:53.314 --> 00:41:54.935
You're taking my workshop, so I can tell you.

00:41:54.935 --> 00:41:56.093
You're giving me license.

00:41:56.093 --> 00:41:58.417
People are not signing up for your workshop.

00:41:58.543 --> 00:42:00.270
Your friends, your roommates, your family

00:42:00.270 --> 00:42:02.298
has not signed up for your workshop.

00:42:02.515 --> 00:42:04.873
Believe me, I've been in this business a long time.

00:42:04.873 --> 00:42:06.610
I used to do BlackRock workshops,

00:42:06.610 --> 00:42:07.690
I've done Essen workshops,

00:42:07.690 --> 00:42:08.864
I've done a lot of workshops.

00:42:08.864 --> 00:42:13.400
You do not want to do this stuff if you're not the guru.

00:42:14.354 --> 00:42:15.863
Fix yourself.

00:42:15.863 --> 00:42:17.634
You can be the guru for yourself.

00:42:17.634 --> 00:42:19.431
Do not be the guru for someone else.

00:42:19.431 --> 00:42:21.303
Just show 'em by example.

00:42:21.303 --> 00:42:23.207
You will ruin your relationships.

00:42:23.207 --> 00:42:25.397
So, seriously speaking, do not,

00:42:25.397 --> 00:42:27.076
thank you, that's a great gift.

00:42:27.076 --> 00:42:30.242
'Cause I mean to say it, and sometimes I forget it.

00:42:30.242 --> 00:42:32.273
And then at night I feel horrible

00:42:32.273 --> 00:42:33.688
what I've created in the world.

00:42:33.688 --> 00:42:34.821
(audience laughs)

00:42:34.821 --> 00:42:36.373
A bunch of monsters, you know what I mean?

00:42:36.603 --> 00:42:37.838
A bunch of monsters.

00:42:37.838 --> 00:42:38.835
Do not do that.

00:42:38.835 --> 00:42:41.613
It really takes something wonderful and nice

00:42:41.613 --> 00:42:43.647
and turns it into something awful.

00:42:43.647 --> 00:42:44.983
Ok, thank you.

00:42:45.753 --> 00:42:49.039
(audience laughs)

00:42:49.349 --> 00:42:51.003
There's one other thing I want to do,

00:42:51.003 --> 00:42:53.311
and that came up with your question there.

00:42:53.311 --> 00:42:56.215
And there's a difference between trying and doing.

00:42:56.215 --> 00:42:58.743
And the thing about it is,

00:42:58.743 --> 00:43:01.161
there's this wonderful line in the Star Wars movie,

00:43:01.161 --> 00:43:02.804
the first one where Yoda,

00:43:02.804 --> 00:43:03.834
and I'm always afraid when I say this

00:43:03.834 --> 00:43:05.685
that I'm gonna be struck down dead,

00:43:05.685 --> 00:43:07.838
but so far I've gotten away with it.

00:43:07.838 --> 00:43:09.575
Yoda was wrong.

00:43:09.797 --> 00:43:13.058
And not in the context that he was doing it, ok.

00:43:13.058 --> 00:43:14.639
But the point is, there is a try.

00:43:14.639 --> 00:43:17.097
He said there's no try, there's just do.

00:43:17.614 --> 00:43:19.577
And, people love to quote that,

00:43:19.577 --> 00:43:22.043
especially entrepreneurs.

00:43:22.400 --> 00:43:25.864
But the point simply is, there's a try and there's a do.

00:43:25.864 --> 00:43:27.909
And they're both ok states.

00:43:28.012 --> 00:43:30.700
There's really nothing wrong with trying to do something.

00:43:30.733 --> 00:43:32.935
And there's certainly nothing wrong with doing it.

00:43:32.935 --> 00:43:36.189
The problem is when you think they're the same thing.

00:43:36.928 --> 00:43:39.558
And when you think you're doing something,

00:43:39.558 --> 00:43:41.555
and you're really just trying.

00:43:41.555 --> 00:43:44.139
And when you're trying, when you hit an obstacle,

00:43:44.139 --> 00:43:46.543
the lamppost, you get defeated.

00:43:47.509 --> 00:43:48.915
And when you're doing,

00:43:48.915 --> 00:43:50.486
it doesn't matter if there's lamppost.

00:43:50.486 --> 00:43:53.751
You jump over it, you knock it down, or you walk around it.

00:43:54.298 --> 00:43:55.604
So there are obstacles.

00:43:55.604 --> 00:43:58.495
I had a fellow who's sort of dear to my life

00:43:58.495 --> 00:44:01.091
in that he's a great story.

00:44:02.146 --> 00:44:04.342
He was a Stanford student in the days

00:44:04.342 --> 00:44:06.370
when I got here way back when.

00:44:06.444 --> 00:44:07.565
And he was a swimmer

00:44:07.565 --> 00:44:09.645
and he had a bad accident in the shower.

00:44:10.101 --> 00:44:12.693
And he had to have a back surgery.

00:44:12.942 --> 00:44:15.401
And the doctors told him he'd never swim again.

00:44:15.581 --> 00:44:17.481
And he wanted to go to the Olympics.

00:44:17.481 --> 00:44:19.823
So he ignored the doctor's advice.

00:44:19.920 --> 00:44:22.676
He got some guy to take him down to Saratoga Reservoir,

00:44:22.676 --> 00:44:23.921
train the hell out of him.

00:44:23.921 --> 00:44:27.103
He just qualified, just barely qualified.

00:44:27.289 --> 00:44:30.001
He went to Rome and he won a gold medal.

00:44:30.104 --> 00:44:31.663
And he came back and he put the gold medal

00:44:31.663 --> 00:44:33.527
on the doctor's desk.

00:44:34.206 --> 00:44:37.338
And he set the world record also, at the time.

00:44:37.728 --> 00:44:39.497
And he told me, he was just down here.

00:44:39.497 --> 00:44:41.753
He's become a lifelong friend.

00:44:41.753 --> 00:44:44.224
And he's a serial entrepreneur.

00:44:44.224 --> 00:44:46.743
He's the most entrepreneurial guy I've ever met.

00:44:47.406 --> 00:44:50.463
And he did pemmican beef jerky, maybe a product you know,

00:44:50.463 --> 00:44:51.375
or you don't know,

00:44:51.465 --> 00:44:53.466
which was a really good product.

00:44:53.759 --> 00:44:55.205
And he told me, "You know, Bernie,

00:44:55.205 --> 00:44:59.868
"every time I have hit a block, that's what got me

00:44:59.868 --> 00:45:02.580
"to do something really great and creative."

00:45:03.090 --> 00:45:04.719
He said, "Everything in my life,"

00:45:04.719 --> 00:45:06.372
It was like the swimming thing.

00:45:06.636 --> 00:45:08.835
"I probably would have never gotten there,

00:45:08.835 --> 00:45:10.692
"if I hadn't gone through that surgery

00:45:10.692 --> 00:45:12.187
"and that whole accident."

00:45:12.187 --> 00:45:13.384
And even, there's another story about

00:45:13.384 --> 00:45:14.980
how he got the event and all that.

00:45:14.980 --> 00:45:18.189
And he has like 20 things he's done,

00:45:18.189 --> 00:45:20.647
and they're really very good things in the world.

00:45:20.647 --> 00:45:23.260
And he said, "Every time there's been something

00:45:23.260 --> 00:45:26.103
"I've just had to go round, that's what made me creative

00:45:26.103 --> 00:45:27.452
"and let me see that."

00:45:27.452 --> 00:45:30.536
So the whole idea is, if you're doing something,

00:45:30.536 --> 00:45:32.152
you're gonna do it no matter what.

00:45:32.152 --> 00:45:34.493
And just having a block can be a gift.

00:45:34.493 --> 00:45:36.454
Having a block can be a big gift,

00:45:36.454 --> 00:45:37.889
'cause it gets you to see something

00:45:37.889 --> 00:45:39.807
that you wouldn't have seen, ok.

00:45:40.780 --> 00:45:44.097
But even if it isn't a gift, you still have to go around it.

00:45:44.097 --> 00:45:46.238
So the whole idea is not to do trying and doing.

00:45:46.238 --> 00:45:47.313
And I'll tell you one story.

00:45:47.313 --> 00:45:49.001
I could tell you this 'cause my wife isn't here,

00:45:49.001 --> 00:45:50.933
and she won't watch it on the tube.

00:45:50.933 --> 00:45:53.814
And she hates when I tell this story.

00:45:53.814 --> 00:45:57.834
However, to me it's the one story which really says it all.

00:45:58.117 --> 00:45:59.385
And the story is the following.

00:45:59.385 --> 00:46:02.427
We were up in San Francisco visiting my son.

00:46:02.427 --> 00:46:04.329
And we were about to drive home.

00:46:04.329 --> 00:46:05.839
And we passed the Roxie Theater,

00:46:05.839 --> 00:46:07.299
which is in the Mission District,

00:46:07.299 --> 00:46:09.678
and it's a kind of funky theater,

00:46:09.678 --> 00:46:12.140
and it shows things you'd never see elsewhere.

00:46:12.140 --> 00:46:13.367
And the line was bigger

00:46:13.367 --> 00:46:15.712
than I've ever seen in front of the Roxie.

00:46:15.712 --> 00:46:17.746
And I noticed it had a band.

00:46:17.772 --> 00:46:19.052
It was a movie about a band,

00:46:19.052 --> 00:46:20.346
and the band was there.

00:46:20.346 --> 00:46:21.395
I'd never heard of them, but I think,

00:46:21.395 --> 00:46:22.937
"This is hot. We've gotta go."

00:46:23.030 --> 00:46:23.920
So I said, "Let's go."

00:46:23.920 --> 00:46:26.166
She said, "No, no, no. I'm tired. Let's go home."

00:46:26.166 --> 00:46:27.152
I said, "Come on, let's go."

00:46:27.152 --> 00:46:28.197
She said, "Ok, we'll go."

00:46:28.197 --> 00:46:30.606
I said, "Jump out. You buy the tickets,

00:46:30.606 --> 00:46:32.170
"and I'll go park the car."

00:46:32.170 --> 00:46:33.778
So I come back ten minutes later,

00:46:33.778 --> 00:46:35.694
and she's not on line.

00:46:36.058 --> 00:46:38.614
And I said, "Well, why aren't you on line?"

00:46:38.614 --> 00:46:40.080
She said, "I couldn't get tickets."

00:46:40.080 --> 00:46:41.250
I said,"Why not? Why couldn't you get tickets?"

00:46:41.250 --> 00:46:43.170
She said, "They're sold out."

00:46:44.713 --> 00:46:45.933
"Yeah, but why couldn't you get tickets?"

00:46:45.933 --> 00:46:47.924
"They're sold out. Didn't you hear me?"

00:46:48.500 --> 00:46:49.817
"Stay here for a few minutes."

00:46:49.817 --> 00:46:51.599
So I went out and I got two tickets.

00:46:51.599 --> 00:46:53.405
So the point was, and she was right.

00:46:53.405 --> 00:46:53.983
It was terrible.

00:46:53.983 --> 00:46:55.059
We shouldn't have gone.

00:46:55.059 --> 00:46:58.245
But the point simply was, she was trying.

00:46:58.428 --> 00:47:00.146
She was accommodating me.

00:47:00.146 --> 00:47:01.591
And she hit an obstacle.

00:47:01.591 --> 00:47:03.439
They were sold out, which was true.

00:47:03.439 --> 00:47:04.512
And that was it.

00:47:04.512 --> 00:47:06.807
She was defeated, and it was ok.

00:47:06.807 --> 00:47:08.195
She didn't mind. She didn't mind.

00:47:08.195 --> 00:47:08.893
She tried.

00:47:08.893 --> 00:47:11.251
And that's the whole idea of trying.

00:47:11.251 --> 00:47:12.037
If it works, fine.

00:47:12.037 --> 00:47:12.793
She would have gone.

00:47:12.793 --> 00:47:15.245
If she would have bought two tickets, we would have gone.

00:47:15.417 --> 00:47:16.912
But she couldn't, but she didn't.

00:47:16.912 --> 00:47:17.934
So trying is that way.

00:47:17.934 --> 00:47:18.551
If it works, fine.

00:47:18.551 --> 00:47:19.609
If it doesn't work, it doesn't.

00:47:19.609 --> 00:47:21.034
Doing is different.

00:47:21.034 --> 00:47:23.776
Doing is, if they're sold out it doesn't matter.

00:47:23.902 --> 00:47:25.496
It doesn't matter if they're sold out,

00:47:25.496 --> 00:47:27.767
you're gonna get two tickets, ok.

00:47:27.957 --> 00:47:29.192
And it's a good example.

00:47:29.192 --> 00:47:30.473
Now I have trying in my life.

00:47:30.473 --> 00:47:33.153
I'm not a jerk like some people, who, they only do.

00:47:33.229 --> 00:47:35.707
If I have to kill him, I'll change my mind.

00:47:35.707 --> 00:47:36.287
I will not,

00:47:36.287 --> 00:47:36.893
Don't worry.

00:47:36.893 --> 00:47:37.903
(audience laughs)

00:47:37.903 --> 00:47:40.659
I'm gonna change my mind from doing to trying, ok.

00:47:40.824 --> 00:47:42.464
But the whole idea is, I had an episode.

00:47:42.464 --> 00:47:44.465
I was supposed to go to a meeting in Texas.

00:47:44.465 --> 00:47:48.472
And I really was reluctant to go, and it was my obligation,

00:47:48.472 --> 00:47:50.288
'cause they had given me some money.

00:47:50.588 --> 00:47:52.169
So I went to the airport,

00:47:52.169 --> 00:47:54.422
and I met my good friend Christa Donaldson there.

00:47:54.422 --> 00:47:55.657
We both were there.

00:47:55.657 --> 00:47:58.235
And the gods were good.

00:47:58.406 --> 00:48:01.799
Dallas-Fort Worth was closed because of snowstorms.

00:48:02.387 --> 00:48:04.267
So I called up and said, "Really sorry.

00:48:04.267 --> 00:48:05.742
"I can't come. It's a snowstorm."

00:48:05.742 --> 00:48:07.119
And they said, "That's ok."

00:48:07.119 --> 00:48:08.972
And we both went to our homes,

00:48:08.972 --> 00:48:10.891
and we were as happy as could be.

00:48:10.891 --> 00:48:11.821
So that was great.

00:48:11.821 --> 00:48:14.192
But if I really wanted to get there,

00:48:14.192 --> 00:48:16.677
do you think having a snowstorm in Dallas-Fort Worth

00:48:16.677 --> 00:48:19.371
would have stopped me from getting to Dallas-Fort Worth?

00:48:19.599 --> 00:48:22.911
If my life depended on getting to Dallas-Fort Worth,

00:48:23.471 --> 00:48:24.984
I would have gotten there.

00:48:26.309 --> 00:48:27.757
So trying is great at times.

00:48:27.757 --> 00:48:28.646
I'm not knocking it.

00:48:28.646 --> 00:48:30.476
That's where Yoda was wrong.

00:48:30.476 --> 00:48:31.736
But you have to be appropriate,

00:48:31.736 --> 00:48:33.794
and you have to know when to stop trying,

00:48:33.794 --> 00:48:36.464
if you're not doing it, and start doing.

00:48:36.464 --> 00:48:39.194
Or forget about the whole thing and do something else.

00:48:39.636 --> 00:48:41.662
So I have to leave a little time for questions.

00:48:41.662 --> 00:48:42.786
I must leave--

00:48:42.786 --> 00:48:43.349
- [Voiceover] Four minutes.

00:48:43.349 --> 00:48:44.704
- Four minutes for questions.

00:48:44.704 --> 00:48:45.401
That's more than enough.

00:48:45.401 --> 00:48:46.892
We've been doing so many questions.

00:48:46.892 --> 00:48:49.912
So, thank you. Let's have questions.

00:48:50.482 --> 00:48:55.339
(audience applauds)

00:48:57.038 --> 00:48:58.179
Yes?

00:48:59.007 --> 00:49:02.448
- [Voiceover] Can you give some, just some thoughts on

00:49:02.448 --> 00:49:04.382
how to change the habits, because--

00:49:04.382 --> 00:49:05.073
- Sure.

00:49:05.073 --> 00:49:07.827
- [Voiceover] we all have a habit of placing ourselves so--

00:49:07.827 --> 00:49:09.484
- Absolutely. It's a great question.

00:49:09.484 --> 00:49:11.082
How do you change the habit?

00:49:11.096 --> 00:49:14.160
It's really hard to do it at one time.

00:49:14.840 --> 00:49:17.252
It's, it's, it's, it's,

00:49:17.762 --> 00:49:20.020
I'll tell you, a woman did a story in the New York Times

00:49:20.020 --> 00:49:21.622
about this, I'll just answer,

00:49:21.622 --> 00:49:23.953
and she called me up and she said,

00:49:23.953 --> 00:49:25.683
"We want to do a project for New Year's,

00:49:25.683 --> 00:49:27.231
"and what should we do?"

00:49:27.231 --> 00:49:30.203
And I said, "Well, the way we do it in the d.school,

00:49:30.203 --> 00:49:32.396
"is we give the reader something to do."

00:49:32.656 --> 00:49:35.313
And she said, "Well, give me an example."

00:49:35.313 --> 00:49:37.525
So I said, "Well, give me something in your life

00:49:37.525 --> 00:49:40.773
"that's really hard to do."

00:49:41.145 --> 00:49:43.613
She said, "Well, to tell you the truth,

00:49:43.613 --> 00:49:45.342
"I've gained a lot of weight, and I'm embarrassed

00:49:45.342 --> 00:49:47.996
"to go out and see my friends" and all that.

00:49:48.108 --> 00:49:50.901
And I said, "Well, what would it do for you if,

00:49:50.901 --> 00:49:52.563
"what would it do for you

00:49:52.563 --> 00:49:56.686
"if you lost the weight?"

00:49:57.788 --> 00:49:59.769
"I'd be able to have a social life."

00:49:59.896 --> 00:50:01.531
So I said, "Well, the problem I would give you

00:50:01.531 --> 00:50:03.755
"as a reader is, go have a social life."

00:50:05.985 --> 00:50:07.493
Nothing happens.

00:50:08.202 --> 00:50:10.754
Sometime later, the article comes out.

00:50:10.754 --> 00:50:14.577
"I lost 25 pounds doing this design thinking stuff."

00:50:14.689 --> 00:50:16.747
So I wrote her a note and I said,

00:50:16.867 --> 00:50:18.266
"Thank you very much."

00:50:18.266 --> 00:50:20.225
She says, "I'm a believer."

00:50:20.597 --> 00:50:22.138
So, in her case, she changed the habit

00:50:22.138 --> 00:50:24.266
by reframing the problem.

00:50:24.670 --> 00:50:27.568
However, the way it generally works is,

00:50:27.568 --> 00:50:28.270
you do it a little,

00:50:28.270 --> 00:50:30.101
like my lateness thing, you know.

00:50:30.101 --> 00:50:32.296
It didn't happen, like, instantly.

00:50:32.296 --> 00:50:33.177
It's just you say,

00:50:33.177 --> 00:50:34.916
"I don't want to have that habit anymore."

00:50:34.916 --> 00:50:36.788
We all have bad habits.

00:50:36.788 --> 00:50:38.750
And if you tell yourself the truth

00:50:38.750 --> 00:50:42.186
and don't give yourself excuses, reasons,

00:50:42.730 --> 00:50:44.835
you will change if you want to.

00:50:45.194 --> 00:50:47.496
It's just getting out of bed, you know what I mean?

00:50:47.956 --> 00:50:50.359
You think he could get out of bed if he wants to?

00:50:51.233 --> 00:50:52.543
No matter what his history is.

00:50:52.543 --> 00:50:53.292
I used to have a friend.

00:50:53.292 --> 00:50:55.772
He used to train people on giving up smoking.

00:50:55.772 --> 00:50:59.101
And he'd get a big auditorium like this, and he'd say,

00:50:59.581 --> 00:51:02.015
and it had "No Smoking" signs around, and he'd go,

00:51:02.015 --> 00:51:03.744
"How many of you are smokers?"

00:51:03.744 --> 00:51:05.247
And they'd all raise their hands.

00:51:05.247 --> 00:51:06.260
And he says, "Well, I don't see anyone

00:51:06.260 --> 00:51:08.311
"with a cigarette in their mouth."

00:51:08.661 --> 00:51:11.312
The point was, that was their perception of themselves.

00:51:11.312 --> 00:51:13.618
But they weren't smokers at that moment.

00:51:13.618 --> 00:51:15.678
And that's the whole thing with you.

00:51:15.678 --> 00:51:18.409
You have a perception of yourself with the bad habit.

00:51:18.438 --> 00:51:23.024
You can change it if you do it a step at a time

00:51:23.024 --> 00:51:24.428
and forgive yourself.

00:51:24.428 --> 00:51:26.184
You're not gonna change it immediately,

00:51:26.184 --> 00:51:27.609
so you're gonna screw up.

00:51:28.499 --> 00:51:30.576
I would use a different word, but we're on TV.

00:51:30.576 --> 00:51:32.154
You would screw up, ok?

00:51:32.264 --> 00:51:34.074
But it doesn't matter. It's ok.

00:51:34.314 --> 00:51:35.886
Now next time, you'll do it.

00:51:35.886 --> 00:51:38.147
And you will do it if you want to do it.

00:51:38.147 --> 00:51:39.656
I guarantee you that.

00:51:40.066 --> 00:51:41.132
If you want to do it,

00:51:41.132 --> 00:51:43.142
if he really wants to get out of bed,

00:51:43.142 --> 00:51:45.600
if she really wants to figure out how to collect her money,

00:51:45.700 --> 00:51:47.210
you can do it.

00:51:47.355 --> 00:51:49.672
Not necessarily the first time, but you can do it.

00:51:49.672 --> 00:51:51.043
So, yeah, we all have bad habits,

00:51:51.043 --> 00:51:52.150
I have 'em, you have 'em.

00:51:52.150 --> 00:51:52.630
- So Bernie,

00:51:52.630 --> 00:51:53.469
- That's what makes you human.

00:51:53.469 --> 00:51:54.491
Yes? We're done.

00:51:54.491 --> 00:51:55.715
- This was so wonderful.

00:51:55.715 --> 00:51:57.657
Please join me in thanking Bernie.

00:51:57.657 --> 00:51:59.047
(audience applause)

00:51:59.047 --> 00:51:59.495
- Thank you.

00:51:59.495 --> 00:52:01.786
(audience applause)

